From 037dabf30585b0819854251fbbdb470446573836 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jky72 <jky72@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 09:11:28 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix(terminal): vendor a patched core so ConPTY sync
 updates always flush

pi's TUI was receiving keystrokes but never redrawing them, which made the terminal
look like it ignored input. The bytes were there: pi's own output shows the DEC 2026
end marker followed by the content it belongs to

    ESC[?2026h ESC[?2026l ESC[16;1H "hello kitty" ...

and the core only looked for the end marker in the last 32 bytes of the receive
buffer. ConPTY appends the payload after the marker, so the marker slid out of that
window and the update buffer was never flushed - permanently frozen, one repaint
behind.

Patch the core through [patch.crates-io] to a vendored copy whose search starts at
`old_len - (marker_len - 1)`, i.e. the newly arrived chunk plus enough overlap for a
marker split across reads. Rescanning the whole buffer instead would make long
updates quadratic.

The vendored tree is the crates.io 0.48.0 layout minus docs/benches-free extras;
`benches/` is kept because Cargo.toml declares a [[bench]] target that points at it
(dropping the file alone breaks `cargo bench` and `--all-targets`).

Add a PTY-level harness (examples/handoff_probe.rs) that replays the captured
ConPTY bytes without any GUI, plus a regression test covering every chunk boundary
the end marker can be split across.

Also move [profile.release] to thin LTO with 16 codegen units and keep the previous
fat-LTO settings under `release-fat`. A one-line change under src/ used to cost
~5m40s on a 32-core machine while using two cores, because fat LTO plus a single
codegen unit serialises the expensive pass for every binary. Thin LTO is within a
few percent of fat LTO; use `--profile release-fat` when cutting a release or when a
timing has to stay comparable with the numbers recorded in the handoff docs.
